    "available_at": "2026-10-27T15:43:56.358527+00:00",
    "body": "Police recovered approximately $54,000 in stolen Hot Wheels merchandise following a freight train burglary in Colton, California. A witness called 911 around 3 a.m. Thursday, October 22, reporting suspicious activity near railroad tracks. Officers spotted a black Chevrolet Silverado leaving the area and initiated a traffic stop. The investigation ended with one arrest and 350 boxes of toy cars in police custody.\nAccording to video released by the Colton Police Department , the caller reported people stealing merchandise near Maple Street. An officer positioned himself near Seventh Street and La Cadena Drive while monitoring the surrounding roads. Footage shows authorities following the pickup before stopping its driver. The man exited with his hands on his head, and officers took him into custody.\nThe department confirmed the recovery in its October 22 Facebook announcement. Authorities booked the suspect into West Valley Detention Center on suspicion of burglary, grand theft and possession of stolen property. The department did not identify the individual in its public announcement.\nIn a response to FreightWaves, Colton Police Department representative Jillian Nunez provided additional details about the investigation. Officers discovered an open Maersk shipping container and additional merchandise along nearby railroad tracks. Investigators identified Mattel as the cargo owner, while BNSF Railway took possession of the recovered property. Nunez confirmed that authorities will return the merchandise to its owner through the appropriate process.\nColton police documented boxes of recovered Hot Wheels merchandise following the October 22 burglary. (Photo: Colton Police Department)\nAnother rail cargo theft in Southern California\nThe Colton, California, arrest follows several freight theft investigations involving BNSF Railway shipments across Southern California. In August, deputies recovered $150,000 in New Balance shoes following a boxcar burglary near Daggett, California. In September, a container caught fire during a burglary aboard a moving BNSF train near Riverside, California . In October, Los Angeles detectives uncovered $1 million in stolen BNSF and Union Pacific merchandise at a Whittier, California, retailer.\nThe Colton Police Department maintains an active enforcement contract with BNSF Railway to address cargo theft and related criminal activity. Nunez confirmed the agreement in her response to FreightWaves. Officials did not disclose the contract’s duration or specific enforcement responsibilities.\nA closer look at cartons of Hot Wheels merchandise recovered during the October 22 investigation in Colton, California. (Photo: Colton Police Department)\nInvestigation leaves questions about container access\nColton police believe additional individuals may have participated in the theft. Nunez confirmed that detectives reached that assessment through witness statements and information gathered during the investigation. Authorities continue examining the circumstances surrounding the burglary under case number 26-07172. The department declined to speculate about possible connections to other Inland Empire cargo thefts.\nInvestigators have not confirmed whether thieves accessed the container while the train was moving or stationary. Police also have not explained how suspects opened the equipment or whether they recovered every missing item. Authorities have not disclosed the shipment’s intended destination. FreightWaves contacted BNSF Railway for additional comment, but the railroad had not responded before publication.\nWhy it matters\nA witness’s 911 call helped officers intercept a vehicle carrying $54,000 in stolen merchandise. The incident highlights the importance of timely reporting and coordinated responses to suspected rail cargo theft.\nClick here for more articles on cargo theft and freight fraud by Phil Brink.\nFeds charge driver in alleged $12M cargo theft scheme built on hijacked carrier identities – FreightWaves\nNearly $1M in Pokémon and sports cards vanish from FedEx truck, driver discovers missing lock – FreightWaves\nFailed CDL exams, $3,000 payments and Bluetooth cheating scheme lead to federal charges – FreightWaves\nUpcoming FreightWaves Events\nFreightTech\nF3: Future of Freight Festival\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nOctober 27, 2026 – October 28, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nFreightTech F3: Future of Freight Festival Oct 27 – Oct 28 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now",
